// Output filename and BoardLoader data-variable name:
//   boardName "RP2040" -> designer/boards/RP2040/RP2040.json -> const RP2040Data = {...}
//
// pfodWeb NOTE (button/LED/NeoPixel + dedicated-hardware pin-exclusion
// audit, 2026-07-13):
//
// Board: SparkFun Thing Plus - RP2040 (plain, non-WiFi RP2040; the
// WiFi-equipped sibling is the RP2350-based variants/rp2040/rp2040w/
// sparkfun_thingplusrp2350 already audited on 2026-07-12). Confirmed via
// boards.txt (`sparkfun_thingplusrp2040.name=SparkFun Thing Plus RP2040`,
// `build.usb_product="Thing Plus RP2040"`, `build.board=SPARKFUN_THINGPLUS_RP2040`)
// that this directory is the genuine Thing Plus RP2040, despite this
// board's own pins_arduino.h citing a "ProMicroRP2040_Graphical_Datasheet.pdf"
// source comment (that citation appears to be a copy/paste carried over
// from arduino-pico upstream and does not match this board's actual
// pin functions confirmed below - not corrected here per task scope,
// pins_arduino.h left untouched).
//
// No board.json override existed before this audit. This board declares
// its own D0-D22/A0-A2/MOSI/MISO/SCK/SDA/SCL aliases directly (does not
// include ../generic/common.h), so without a per-board override every
// aliased GPIO defaults to full digital_input/digital_output/pwm_output
// via build_boards.js's buildEsp32Board() - including six GPIOs
// dedicated internally to the onboard microSD card slot, which is not
// safe or meaningful for a sketch to twiddle as plain digital I/O.
//
// Sources consulted:
//  - This board's own pins_arduino.h (variants/rp2040/rp2040/
//    sparkfun_thingplusrp2040/pins_arduino.h): `PIN_LED (25u)`,
//    `PIN_NEOPIXEL (8u)` / `NUM_NEOPIXEL (1)`, `PIN_SPI1_MISO (12u)` /
//    `PIN_SPI1_MOSI (15u)` / `PIN_SPI1_SCK (14u)` / `PIN_SPI1_SS (9u)`.
//    Authoritative - this is the actual arduino-pico board-support
//    source the generated sketches compile against.
//  - variants/rp2040/boards.txt (`sparkfun_thingplusrp2040.*` block):
//    confirms this variant maps to the real "SparkFun Thing Plus RP2040"
//    product (name=, build.usb_product=, build.board=).
//  - https://learn.sparkfun.com/tutorials/rp2040-thing-plus-hookup-guide/all
//    and .../hardware-overview - Official SparkFun hookup guide.
//    Verbatim: "The µSD card slot is connected to the following
//    dedicated GPIO: GPIO 09: DATA3/CS, GPIO 10: DATA2, GPIO 11: DATA1,
//    GPIO 12: DATA0/CIPO (or Peripheral's SDO), GPIO 14: CLK/SCK,
//    GPIO 15: CMD/COPI (or Peripheral's SDI)." Also: "30x 3.3V
//    user-programmable high-speed IO (only 18 are broken out on the
//    board)" - the 12 GPIOs NOT broken out are exactly GPIO6/7 (I2C1
//    Qwiic + fuel-gauge SCL tie), GPIO8 (WS2812), GPIO9/10/11/12/14/15
//    (µSD, all six explicitly called "dedicated"), GPIO23 (tied SCL,
//    no pins_arduino.h alias at all so already excluded automatically),
//    GPIO24 (MAX17048 fuel-gauge alert, likewise no pins_arduino.h
//    alias), GPIO25 (status LED) - confirming all six µSD GPIOs are
//    internal/dedicated, not header-broken-out shared pins (unlike the
//    RP2350 sibling's SPI0, which IS shared with the header).
//    Status LED: "The blue, 25 LED is typically used as a test or
//    status LED... connected to GPIO 25." NeoPixel: "WS2812 RGB LED:
//    Connected to GPIO 8; controlled with a 24-bit (GRB) data signal."
//    Buttons: "BOOT Button: Forces BOOTSEL mode when held during USB
//    connection" / "RESET Button: Connected to reset pin" - both
//    hardware-only, not normal GPIOs, matching every other RP2040
//    board's BOOTSEL/RESET pair - out of scope for GPIO tagging.
//  - MicroPython example on the same hookup guide page for the status
//    LED: `led.value(1)` turns it on - confirms active-high polarity
//    (Pin.value(1) drives the pin HIGH).
//  - Closely related sibling boards already audited in this repo:
//    variants/rp2040/rp2040w/sparkfun_thingplusrp2350/board.json (same
//    product line, SPI0-vs-SD-sharing precedent) and variants/rp2040/
//    rp2040/sparkfun_promicrorp2040/board.json (same
//    "ProMicroRP2040_Graphical_Datasheet.pdf" source citation quirk,
//    confirms that citation is a red herring shared across multiple
//    distinct SparkFun boards' pins_arduino.h comments, not evidence
//    this board is actually a Pro Micro).
//
// GPIO8 (PIN_NEOPIXEL): CONFIRMED onboard WS2812 addressable RGB LED
// data line, vendor-documented and confirmed in pins_arduino.h
// (NUM_NEOPIXEL=1). Tagged ["digital_output","led_neopixel","pwm_output"],
// matching this repo's existing led_neopixel precedent (sibling
// sparkfun_thingplusrp2350 GPIO14, sparkfun_promicrorp2040 GPIO25).
// digital_input dropped - the pin is committed to driving the LED, not
// general-purpose input.
//
// GPIO9/10/11/12/14/15 (PIN_SPI1_SS/D10/D11/PIN_SPI1_MISO/PIN_SPI1_SCK/
// PIN_SPI1_MOSI): CONFIRMED onboard microSD card slot's six dedicated
// signal lines (CS/DATA3, DATA2, DATA1, DATA0-CIPO, CLK-SCK,
// CMD-COPI), vendor-documented as "dedicated GPIO" and explicitly
// excluded from this board's 18 header-broken-out pins (see sources
// above) - unlike the RP2350 sibling board, where the microSD's SPI0
// bus is also broken out to the header and left general-purpose, this
// board's SD-dedicated SPI1 bus is NOT header-broken-out at all, so all
// six GPIOs get capabilities suppressed (empty), not just the CS pin.
// build_boards.js's unconditional SS/MISO/SCK/MOSI-alias match still
// attaches the correct spi_ss/spi_miso/spi_sck/spi_mosi bus-role tags
// on top of GPIO9/12/14/15 (that vocabulary isn't part of chipGpios'
// CAP_ORDER, so it can't be suppressed and shouldn't be - it correctly
// reflects the pins' real SPI role); GPIO10/11 (DATA2/DATA1) have no
// SPI-role alias at all in pins_arduino.h (plain D10/D11 only), so they
// simply get no capabilities and no bus-role tag.
//
// GPIO25 (PIN_LED): CONFIRMED onboard blue status/debug LED, vendor-
// documented ("The blue, 25 LED is typically used as a test or status
// LED... connected to GPIO 25") and confirmed in pins_arduino.h. Tagged
// ["digital_output","pwm_output","led_high"] - active-high confirmed via
// the hookup guide's own MicroPython example (`led.value(1)` to turn
// on). digital_input dropped to match this repo's established led_high
// precedent for dedicated onboard LED pins (rpipico, wiznet_5500_evb_pico,
// vccgnd_yd_rp2040, etc.).
//
// GPIO6/7 (Wire1 SDA/SCL): confirmed Qwiic I2C1 connector pins (also
// shared internally with the MAX17048 fuel gauge on the same bus) -
// already correctly tagged via automatic SDA/SCL alias matching, no
// override needed, matching the identical RP2350-sibling precedent for
// its own Qwiic pins.
//
// Not investigated further (out of scope for this per-board audit,
// no pins_arduino.h alias exists so no capability leak is possible):
// GPIO23 (tied SCL half of the I2C1 pair) and GPIO24 (MAX17048 fuel
// gauge ALERT pin) - neither has any D<n>/bus alias in this board's own
// pins_arduino.h, so build_boards.js's alias-driven capability grant
// never fires for them regardless of chip-level defaults.
//
// See boardsDetails/rp2040/rp2040/sparkfun_thingplusrp2040/notes.txt
// for the full research trail.
{
  "boardName": "RP2040",
  "displayName": "Raspberry Pi Pico (RP2040)",
  "family": "rp2040",
  "inputOnlyGpios": [],
  "connections": ["serial"],
  "pinNotes": {},
  "chipGpios": {
    "8":  { "capabilities": ["digital_output", "led_neopixel", "pwm_output"], "note": "PIN_NEOPIXEL - onboard WS2812 addressable RGB LED data line (NUM_NEOPIXEL=1). Confirmed via SparkFun's official RP2040 Thing Plus Hookup Guide (\"WS2812 RGB LED: Connected to GPIO 8\") and this board's own pins_arduino.h." },
    "9":  { "capabilities": [], "note": "PIN_SPI1_SS - onboard microSD card slot CS/DATA3 line. Vendor-documented as one of six \"dedicated GPIO\" for the µSD slot, NOT among the board's 18 header-broken-out pins (docs.sparkfun.com hardware-overview). build_boards.js's automatic SS-alias match still attaches spi_ss on top." },
    "10": { "capabilities": [], "note": "D10 - onboard microSD card slot DATA2 line. Vendor-documented dedicated µSD GPIO, not header-broken-out. No SPI-role alias in pins_arduino.h, so no bus-role tag applies either." },
    "11": { "capabilities": [], "note": "D11 - onboard microSD card slot DATA1 line. Vendor-documented dedicated µSD GPIO, not header-broken-out. No SPI-role alias in pins_arduino.h, so no bus-role tag applies either." },
    "12": { "capabilities": [], "note": "PIN_SPI1_MISO - onboard microSD card slot DATA0/CIPO line. Vendor-documented dedicated µSD GPIO, not header-broken-out. build_boards.js's automatic MISO-alias match still attaches spi_miso on top." },
    "14": { "capabilities": [], "note": "PIN_SPI1_SCK - onboard microSD card slot CLK/SCK line. Vendor-documented dedicated µSD GPIO, not header-broken-out. build_boards.js's automatic SCK-alias match still attaches spi_sck on top." },
    "15": { "capabilities": [], "note": "PIN_SPI1_MOSI - onboard microSD card slot CMD/COPI line. Vendor-documented dedicated µSD GPIO, not header-broken-out. build_boards.js's automatic MOSI-alias match still attaches spi_mosi on top." },
    "25": { "capabilities": ["digital_output", "pwm_output", "led_high"], "note": "PIN_LED - onboard blue status/debug LED, active-high. Confirmed via SparkFun's official RP2040 Thing Plus Hookup Guide (\"The blue, 25 LED is typically used as a test or status LED... connected to GPIO 25\"; MicroPython example uses led.value(1) to turn it on) and this board's own pins_arduino.h." }
  },
  "pwm": { "min": 0, "max": 255, "method": "analogWrite" },
  "dac": {},
  "adc": { "bits": 12, "max": 4095, "defaultRefVolts": "3.3" }
}
